export const ARTICLES_DB: Article[] = [
  { id: 1, title: 'The Art of the False Nine: Tactical Evolution in Modern Football', author: 'Marcus Tactico', date: '2024-01-15', summary: 'How the false nine role has transformed attacking play across Europe\'s top leagues.', content: 'The false nine is one of football\'s most fascinating tactical innovations. Popularized by Lionel Messi under Pep Guardiola at Barcelona, this role sees a center-forward drop deep into midfield, creating numerical superiority and dragging center-backs out of position.\n\nIn recent seasons, we\'ve seen variations of this role employed by managers across Europe. Roberto Firmino was instrumental in Liverpool\'s success, while more recently, players like Phil Foden and Bernardo Silva have adapted to this fluid positioning.\n\nThe tactical implications are profound. When a false nine drops deep, it forces opposition defenders to make a difficult decision: follow the player and leave space behind, or stay in position and allow them time on the ball. This creates the famous "pass before the assist" - the key that unlocks compact defenses.\n\nModern analytics show that teams employing a false nine average 15% more passes in the final third and create 20% more chances from through balls. The role requires exceptional technical ability, tactical intelligence, and work rate - making it one of the most demanding positions in modern football.', tags: ['Tactics', 'Analysis', 'Premier League'], readTime: '8 min' },
  { id: 2, title: 'Pressing Perfection: How the Gegenpress Changed Football', author: 'Sarah Analytics', date: '2024-02-01', summary: 'Jurgen Klopp\'s pressing philosophy and its lasting impact on the modern game.', content: 'The gegenpress, or counter-press, has become the defining tactical approach of the 2020s. Jurgen Klopp\'s Liverpool demonstrated its effectiveness at the highest level, winning the Premier League and Champions League with a relentless, high-energy approach.\n\nThe principle is simple yet devastating: when possession is lost, the team presses immediately to win it back within 5-6 seconds. This prevents the opposition from organizing their attack and often leads to turnovers in dangerous areas.\n\nData from the 2023-24 season shows that the top pressing teams (Liverpool, Tottenham, Newcastle) win possession in the final third an average of 8.5 times per match, compared to 4.2 for teams that sit deeper. This translates directly to more shots and higher expected goals.\n\nHowever, the physical demands are immense. Teams must be meticulously conditioned, and squad rotation becomes essential. The gegenpress also requires specific player profiles - midfielders with explosive acceleration and forwards willing to run themselves into the ground.', tags: ['Tactics', 'Premier League', 'Analysis'], readTime: '7 min' },
  { id: 3, title: 'Youth Development: The Next Generation of Superstars', author: 'James Scout', date: '2024-02-20', summary: 'A deep dive into the academies producing the world\'s best young talent.', content: 'Football academies have evolved dramatically over the past decade. The days of simply identifying talented youngsters are gone - replaced by sophisticated multi-disciplinary programs that combine technical training with psychological support, nutritional planning, and education.\n\nBarcelona\'s La Masia remains the gold standard, having produced Lionel Messi, Xavi, Iniesta, and more recently, Pedri and Gavi. But other clubs are catching up. Bayern Munich\'s campus has produced Jamal Musiala, while Borussia Dortmund has built a reputation for giving young players first-team opportunities.\n\nThe data is compelling. In the 2023-24 season, 23% of minutes in the Champions League were played by players aged 21 or under - up from 15% five years ago. Clubs are increasingly willing to trust young players in high-pressure situations.\n\nWhat separates the best academies? Three factors stand out: (1) clear playing philosophy from academy to first team, (2) qualified coaches at every age group, and (3) a culture that embraces mistakes as learning opportunities. The next generation - featuring players like Lamine Yamal, Endrick, and Arda Guler - promises to be the most technically gifted yet.', tags: ['Youth', 'Development', 'Analysis'], readTime: '9 min' },
  { id: 4, title: 'The Economics of Modern Transfers: How Football Went Crazy', author: 'David Finance', date: '2024-03-05', summary: 'An analysis of the transfer market\'s exponential growth and its implications.', content: 'The summer of 2023 saw football transfer spending reach unprecedented levels. Saudi Arabia\'s entry into the market, combined with continued Premier League dominance, created a perfect storm of inflation.\n\nThe numbers are staggering. Global transfer spending exceeded €10 billion for the first time, with the average Premier League signing costing €45 million. Even mid-table clubs now routinely spend €30-50 million on individual players.\n\nSaudi Arabia\'s Pro League spent over €950 million in 2023, attracting stars like Neymar, Benzema, and Mane. This has created a new dynamic in the market - a third major destination alongside Europe\'s top leagues.\n\nFor UEFA, Financial Fair Play remains a challenge. While the rules have been tightened, creative accounting and commercial deals continue to push boundaries. The gap between leagues is widening - the Premier League\'s broadcasting revenue now exceeds €4 billion annually, more than double La Liga\'s.\n\nWhat does this mean for football\'s competitive balance? The evidence suggests that domestic leagues are becoming more predictable, but European competition remains competitive due to the knockout format.', tags: ['Transfers', 'Finance', 'Analysis'], readTime: '10 min' },
  { id: 5, title: 'Set-Piece Revolution: How Data is Changing Dead Ball Situations', author: 'Elena Setplay', date: '2024-03-18', summary: 'The analytical approach to corners and free kicks transforming the game.', content: 'Set-pieces account for approximately 30% of goals in professional football, yet until recently, they were often treated as an afterthought in training. That has changed dramatically with the advent of specialist coaches and data analysis.\n\nTeams like Liverpool and Manchester City now employ dedicated set-piece coaches. They analyze opponent tendencies, practice specific routines, and track success rates with the same rigor as open play.\n\nThe innovations are fascinating. Teams now use decoy runs, blocking techniques, and carefully choreographed movements to create space. Brentford and Brighton have been particularly successful, scoring 15-20% more set-piece goals than expected based on chance quality.\n\nDefensively, zonal marking has made a comeback, often combined with man-marking in hybrid systems. The key is organization and communication - teams that concede the fewest set-piece goals typically spend the most time practicing these situations.\n\nFor aspiring coaches, set-piece specialization represents a significant career opportunity. As the margins between top teams narrow, these details increasingly decide championships.', tags: ['Tactics', 'Data', 'Analysis'], readTime: '7 min' },
  { id: 6, title: 'Women\'s Football: The Rapid Rise and Bright Future', author: 'Maria Progress', date: '2024-04-01', summary: 'The exponential growth of women\'s football and what comes next.', content: 'Women\'s football has experienced explosive growth over the past five years. The 2023 Women\'s World Cup in Australia and New Zealand was a watershed moment, with record attendance and viewership figures.\n\nThe commercial growth is equally impressive. The Women\'s Super League in England saw broadcasting revenue increase by 300%, while Barcelona\'s women\'s team regularly fills the Camp Nou with over 90,000 fans.\n\nOn the pitch, the quality has improved dramatically. Tactical sophistication now matches the men\'s game, with teams employing complex pressing systems and positional play. The technical level has risen as investment in youth development and coaching has increased.\n\nChallenges remain. Pay equity is a ongoing issue, with most female players earning a fraction of their male counterparts. Infrastructure and accessibility also vary significantly between countries.\n\nHowever, the trajectory is unmistakably upward. FIFA aims to have 60 million female players worldwide by 2026, and with continued investment, women\'s football will become one of the world\'s biggest sports in its own right.', tags: ['Women\'s Football', 'Growth', 'Analysis'], readTime: '8 min' },
  { id: 7, title: 'The Goalkeeper Evolution: From Shot-Stopper to Playmaker', author: 'Tom Keeper', date: '2024-04-15', summary: 'How modern goalkeepers have become football\'s most transformative position.', content: 'The goalkeeper position has undergone the most dramatic transformation of any role in modern football. From primarily shot-stoppers, keepers are now expected to be elite distributors and even participate in build-up play.\n\nEderson at Manchester City and Alisson at Liverpool epitomize this evolution. Both complete more passes per game than many outfield players and are comfortable receiving the ball under pressure. Ederson even has Premier League assists to his name.\n\nThe data tells the story. In 2010, goalkeepers averaged 25 passes per game at 55% accuracy. By 2024, those numbers have shifted to 35 passes at 85% accuracy. The emphasis on distribution has changed recruitment - teams now prioritize keepers with strong footwork and decision-making.\n\nSweeping has also become essential. Keepers must read the game defensively, rushing out to intercept through balls. Manuel Neuer popularized this "sweeper-keeper" role, and it\'s now standard at the elite level.\n\nThe next frontier? Goalkeepers as penalty takers and set-piece threats. We\'ve already seen keepers score from goal kicks and corners. As tactical innovation continues, the goalkeeper\'s role will only expand further.', tags: ['Goalkeepers', 'Tactics', 'Analysis'], readTime: '7 min' },
  { id: 8, title: 'Football Analytics: xG and Beyond', author: 'Dr. Numbers', date: '2024-05-01', summary: 'How advanced statistics are changing how we understand and evaluate football.', content: 'Expected Goals (xG) has become the foundational metric of modern football analytics. But the field has expanded far beyond this single measure, providing unprecedented insight into player and team performance.\n\nKey advanced metrics now include:\n- xA (Expected Assists): Measures the quality of chances created\n- xGOT (Expected Goals on Target): Accounts for shot placement\n- PPDA (Passes Per Defensive Action): Measures pressing intensity\n- OBV (On-Ball Value): Estimates the change in scoring probability from each action\n\nThese metrics have transformed recruitment. Clubs now use data to identify undervalued players and avoid expensive mistakes. Brighton and Brentford are exemplars of this approach, consistently punching above their financial weight.\n\nFor fans, analytics has enriched the viewing experience. Broadcasters now display xG in real-time, and tactical discussions have become more nuanced. However, the human element remains crucial - data enhances but cannot replace traditional scouting and coaching expertise.\n\nThe future lies in tracking data and AI. Player tracking systems now capture every movement on the pitch, enabling analysis of off-ball runs, defensive positioning, and team shape with extraordinary precision.', tags: ['Analytics', 'Data', 'Technology'], readTime: '9 min' },
  { id: 9, title: 'The World Cup 2022: A Tournament for the Ages', author: 'Carlos Mundial', date: '2024-05-20', summary: 'Reliving the drama, surprises, and legacy of Qatar 2022.', content: 'The 2022 FIFA World Cup in Qatar was a tournament of extraordinary drama and significance. From Saudi Arabia\'s stunning upset of Argentina to Morocco\'s historic semi-final run, it delivered moments that will be remembered for generations.\n\nThe final between Argentina and France stands as perhaps the greatest World Cup final ever played. A hat-trick from Kylian Mbappe, a Lionel Messi masterclass, and a penalty shootout that crowned Argentina champions after 36 years.\n\nBeyond the drama, the tournament raised important questions. The scheduling in November-December disrupted European league seasons, while controversies around host selection and labor rights sparked global debate.\n\nOn the pitch, several trends emerged. Compact defensive shapes and rapid transitions proved effective - Morocco and Croatia\'s success demonstrated that organization and mentality can overcome technical inferiority.\n\nFor Messi, it was redemption and coronation. The one missing piece of his legacy was secured, cementing his status alongside Maradona in Argentine football mythology. The image of him lifting the trophy is already iconic.', tags: ['World Cup', 'Argentina', 'History'], readTime: '11 min' },
  { id: 10, title: 'Football in 2030: Predicting the Future of the Beautiful Game', author: 'Future Ball', date: '2024-06-01', summary: 'How technology, AI, and globalization will transform football in the next decade.', content: 'Predicting football\'s future requires understanding the forces shaping its present. Three major trends will define the next decade: technological integration, global expansion, and environmental sustainability.\n\nSemi-automated offside technology, already used in Champions League and World Cup, will become universal. AI-powered analytics will provide real-time tactical recommendations to managers during matches. VAR will evolve to make decisions in seconds rather than minutes.\n\nThe globalization of football will accelerate. The 2026 World Cup in North America will expand to 48 teams, while the 2030 tournament will mark the centenary with a three-continent hosting arrangement. Saudi Arabia\'s investment will likely continue, potentially creating a true global super league.\n\nClimate change will force adaptation. Summer tournaments may shift to spring or autumn, while extreme heat protocols will become standard. Stadium design will prioritize sustainability, with renewable energy and reduced carbon footprints.\n\nOn the pitch, the trend toward athletic, versatile players will continue. Positional fluidity will reach new heights, with formations becoming increasingly dynamic. The next generation - featuring players born in 2010 and beyond - will grow up with analytics and technology as integral parts of their development.', tags: ['Future', 'Technology', 'Analysis'], readTime: '10 min' },
  { id: 11, title: 'Mental Health in Football: Breaking the Silence', author: 'Dr. Wellness', date: '2024-06-15', summary: 'How the football world is finally addressing mental health challenges.', content: 'Football has long been a sport where mental toughness was assumed and mental health struggles were hidden. That is finally changing, as high-profile players speak openly about their experiences.\n\nAndre Gomes, Dele Alli, and Danny Rose are among those who have shared their battles with depression and anxiety. Their courage has helped destigmatize these issues within the sport.\n\nThe pressure on modern players is immense. Social media exposes them to constant criticism, while the 24/7 news cycle leaves no room for privacy. Young players, in particular, struggle with the transition from academy to professional football.\n\nClubs are responding. Most Premier League teams now employ sports psychologists, and the PFA has expanded its mental health support services. The FA has introduced mandatory mental health awareness training for coaches.\n\nThe next step is cultural change. Managers must create environments where players feel safe discussing mental health, just as they would a physical injury. Performance and well-being are not mutually exclusive - research shows that psychologically supported players perform better on the pitch.', tags: ['Mental Health', 'Wellness', 'Analysis'], readTime: '8 min' },
  { id: 12, title: 'The Rise of the Defensive Midfielder: Football\'s Most Underrated Role', author: 'Tactical Deep', date: '2024-06-28', summary: 'Why the CDM has become the most strategically vital position in modern football.', content: 'If you want to understand modern football, study the defensive midfielder. This position has evolved from a simple destroyer to the team\'s tactical quarterback, dictating tempo and controlling space.\n\nRodri\'s role in Manchester City\'s success exemplifies this evolution. Under Pep Guardiola, he operates as a "lone pivot," receiving the ball from center-backs and distributing to advanced players. His passing range and composure under pressure make him irreplaceable.\n\nDifferent managers employ different profiles. At Liverpool, Fabinho (and now Endo) provides a physical screening presence. At Arsenal, Declan Rice combines defensive solidity with ball-carrying ability. At Real Madrid, Aurelien Tchouameni offers athleticism and tactical intelligence.\n\nThe defensive midfielder\'s responsibilities include: (1) collecting the ball from defense, (2) progressing play through lines, (3) covering defensive transitions, and (4) organizing the team\'s pressing shape. No other position demands such a diverse skill set.\n\nRecruitment for this role is increasingly competitive. Elite defensive midfielders command fees exceeding €100 million, reflecting their strategic importance. For young players, mastering this position offers a clear pathway to professional success.', tags: ['Tactics', 'Midfielders', 'Analysis'], readTime: '8 min' },
  { id: 13, title: 'South American Football: The Cradle of Genius', author: 'Latam Ball', date: '2024-07-10', summary: 'The enduring magic and unique characteristics of South American football.', content: 'South American football operates in a different rhythm to its European counterpart. Where European football prioritizes structure and athleticism, South American football celebrates improvisation, individual brilliance, and passion.\n\nThe Copa America 2024 reminded the world of this distinction. The tournament featured tactical innovation - particularly from Argentina and Colombia - alongside the raw emotion that defines the continent\'s football culture.\n\nBrazil and Argentina remain the powerhouses, but other nations are closing the gap. Uruguay\'s youth development under Marcelo Bielsa has produced exciting talents, while Colombia\'s tactical evolution under Nestor Lorenzo has made them genuine contenders.\n\nThe economic disparity between South American and European clubs continues to drive player migration. Top talents leave as teenagers, often before they\'ve played professionally in their home countries. This "brain drain" weakens domestic leagues but enriches European football.\n\nYet the connection between South American communities and their clubs remains unbreakable. The Boca Juniors vs River Plate superclasico, the Fla-Fla derby in Rio, and the Barras bravas supporter cultures represent a football passion that transcends sport.', tags: ['South America', 'Copa America', 'Culture'], readTime: '9 min' },
  { id: 14, title: 'The Saudi Pro League: A New Football Power?', author: 'Middle East', date: '2024-07-20', summary: 'Analyzing the SPL\'s rapid rise and its long-term prospects.', content: 'Saudi Arabia\'s entry into elite football has been unprecedented in its speed and scale. In 18 months, the Saudi Pro League transformed from a regional competition to a global destination for world-class players.\n\nThe numbers are remarkable. Over €2 billion invested in transfer fees and wages. Stars like Cristiano Ronaldo, Neymar, Karim Benzema, and Sadio Mane attracted to the league. Average attendance increased by 150%.\n\nThe strategy mirrors China\'s earlier attempt but with more sustainable foundations. Saudi clubs are state-backed but operate within a structured league system. The investment is part of Vision 2030, the kingdom\'s diversification plan.\n\nOn the pitch, the quality has improved dramatically. Al Hilal reached the Club World Cup final, while Saudi clubs have become competitive in the Asian Champions League. However, the gap with European elite clubs remains significant.\n\nThe long-term question is sustainability. Can the SPL develop local talent alongside imported stars? Will the league maintain interest once the initial novelty fades? The answers will determine whether Saudi Arabia becomes a permanent football power or a fleeting phenomenon.', tags: ['Saudi Arabia', 'Transfers', 'Analysis'], readTime: '10 min' },
  { id: 15, title: 'Football Stadiums: Architecture Meets Passion', author: 'Stadium Arc', date: '2024-08-01', summary: 'The most iconic and innovative football stadiums around the world.', content: 'Football stadiums are more than venues - they are cathedrals of sport, architectural statements, and community gathering places. The best stadiums combine functionality with atmosphere, creating an experience that transcends the match itself.\n\nModern stadium design prioritizes fan experience. Tottenham Hotspur Stadium, opened in 2019, set new standards with its retractable pitch, microbrewery, and 17,500-seat single-tier stand designed to maximize atmosphere.\n\nBarcelona\'s Espai Barca project will redevelop the Camp Nou into a 105,000-capacity all-seater stadium while preserving its iconic bowl shape. Real Madrid\'s renovated Santiago Bernabeu features a retractable roof and underground pitch storage system.\n\nSustainability is increasingly important. Forest Green Rovers\' Eco Park aims to be the world\'s first all-wood stadium, while several German clubs have installed solar panels and rainwater collection systems.\n\nThe most atmospheric stadiums - Anfield, Borussia Dortmund\'s Signal Iduna Park, Boca Juniors\' La Bombonera - share common traits: steep stands close to the pitch, passionate supporters, and decades of history. Modern designers aim to replicate this intensity while meeting contemporary standards.', tags: ['Stadiums', 'Architecture', 'Culture'], readTime: '8 min' }
];

